WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.260
So pins provides a way
to easily share things

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.900
either across projects,
or across people.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.930
What ends up happening
oftentimes in data science

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.480
work, we have some asset.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.650
And maybe it's data,
or it's a model.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.470
Or it's some other R object.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:36.580
And we either need to reuse
this asset later on downstream

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.560
or across different
projects, or we

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.080
might need to share it with
different members of our team.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.400
We say anything that can
be serializable in R.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.820
So think about how you
might stick something

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.090
on a cork board, so
you can keep it handy.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.820
Or you stick a flyer on a
community bulletin board

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.190
at the grocery store, so
that other people can see it.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.140
And so, the pins
package pins objects

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.740
to what's called a board.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.010
This is analogous to
this corkboard idea.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.120
And so, this board is this
place where files are written,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.010
and they're read from.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.160
Examples of boards
are things like, you

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.580
can have a board
that's S3 storage,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.810
shared folders like
network drives or Dropbox.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.770
You can have your RCO
connect be a board,

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.840
a SharePoint site be board.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.050
Really, it's just a
filestore, a file share.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:33.620
Nothing magical about that.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.710
It's just pins has a
nomenclature and a way

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.070
of organizing things in
this place on the board,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.910
so that you can interact with
them through the package.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:46.390
So what I'm hearing is, I
can pin all kinds of things

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.490
in all kinds of locations?

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:56.420
Yes it works
nicely with Connect,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.640
but there's nothing
specifically implemented

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.290
in pins for Connect.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.620
The things that Connect
does to make life

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:11.270
for pins a little bit better,
Connect will give you access

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.280
to the same sharing--

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.350
content sharing
settings that you

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.120
have for any piece of
content on Connect.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.460
So I can put my pin
on Connect, and I

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:25.160
can specify who should be able
to access it and share it.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:32.240
And then, pins does have a nice
preview of the pinned object.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.640
I like that feature, but
that's a Connect implementation

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.960
for how it reads pins.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.030
Nothing specific in the
pins package itself.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.235
So if I work by
myself, either in--

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.800
for whatever reason,
I am a team of one.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.510
How might I use pins?

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.190
A lot comes down to
what are your pain points?

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.200
And what do you have
available to you already

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.670
for storing data, sharing data,
accessing data downstream,

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:08.420
things like that?

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.800
In your team of one, do
you need to reuse something

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.450
in other work?

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.910
Do you have a reference table or
some kind of other information

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.430
that multiple projects
are going to use?

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.320
You might need to
come back to this.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.280
Pins could be a pretty
good use case for that.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:38.000
Or do you just not have a
convenient place to put things?

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.730
Pins comes in and gets
to be really helpful

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:45.170
when you just maybe don't have
another place to store data.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:51.540
Sometimes, teams that don't
have ready access to a database,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.900
find pins to be pretty
helpful because it

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.730
makes them self-sufficient.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.220
It can be more
autonomous in this way.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.712
You don't have the
mechanism to either get

00:04:00.712 --> 00:04:02.420
a hold of a database,
or have one set up.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.095
Or even the data that
you're working with isn't--

00:04:07.095 --> 00:04:08.180
what's the right word?

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.910
--worthy of being
in the database.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.900
Then you can use a
pin as a place to I'm

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.200
going to have this
nice location where

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.050
it's organized, its versioned.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.280
I can work through cached
versions of it as well,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.190
so things go faster.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.380
And it helps to alleviate
other pain points.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.680
If you have places
like if putting things

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.900
in Dropbox, or on GitHub.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.570
If those things are
working for you already,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.780
then you don't need to bring
this tool into your toolbox

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:39.792
per se.

00:04:39.792 --> 00:04:41.000
What pain points do you have?

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.740
And what needs do you
maybe have in terms

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.685
of being able to track versions
or share things more readily?

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.140
That was one of my entry
points for pins, too,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.750
is working with Excel
files just like that.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.120
And what do you do when
you've got this Excel

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.070
file in your workflow?

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.790
At some point, you're going
to get another version of it.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:09.350
And either on the file server,
where the file's stored,

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.770
or in your code, every
time there's a new version,

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.520
you're going to go in
there and rename it.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.280
And so, the final version one,
or latest copy, or whatever.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.540
Final final.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.280
Final final.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.760
With your initials on the end.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.820
And so, somewhere
along the line,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:29.480
you're going to be putting
some kind of hacked versioning

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.200
nomenclature on it.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.340
And there's this introducing
possibility for error,

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:36.590
of course.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:37.970
How many times
have you gone out,

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.140
and you've run your
whole analysis?

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.840
And you realize, oh, I forgot
to change the read underscore

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.086
CSV to the latest version?

00:05:45.086 --> 00:05:50.540
Or do you actually have more
rigor going to the file server

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:53.660
and changing the file name,
so that the latest version

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.060
is always called whatever?

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.120
Data dot CSV, and then
everything else is archived?

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.160
In some form or fashion, you're
implementing this hacky way

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.350
of keeping things current.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:10.490
Whereas with pins, you
can pin that CSV and--

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.770
so pins has different
options for versioning,

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:16.310
but, fundamentally, you
can have pins always

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.080
pull the latest version.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.580
And so, whenever your code
just says pin read data

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.365
from my pin board,
it's always going

00:06:24.365 --> 00:06:25.490
to pull the latest version.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.470
Or if you want to get
specific, you can say,

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:32.540
I want this particular version
always niche every time.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.760
So that even if new
versions come online,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.400
then you're always
referencing the specific one.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.690
Let's say I'm using Dropbox.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:45.800
Do I just get one board?

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.430
Or can I set up different
boards for different projects?

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.980
Or is it like one
board per system?

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.160
Like I get one Dropbox board,
one Google Drive board,

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.780
one Connect board?

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:57.642
Yeah.

00:06:57.642 --> 00:06:59.600
It took me a while to
get my mind around what's

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.550
this thing called a board.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.510
It sounds like there's magic
under the hood or something

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:09.395
really special about boards.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:12.740
There's nothing really super
exciting about boards, per se.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.500
It's a file path basically.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.090
It's a file path.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:24.260
So I have on my screen here--
this is my browser window here.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.560
I started a Dropbox location.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.480
I have a folder called pins.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.390
I manually created this
folder called pins,

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:37.100
and so, earlier today, I
just started pinning stuff.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:41.960
But this is all
that my board is.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.730
My board is a file path.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.050
That's users Katie,
Dropbox, pins.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.830
And this is where
I've defined it.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:51.940
This is my board.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.830
And what happens when
you define a board,

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.070
it just says, this is where
I'm going to go write or read

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:00.470
my files, and where they live.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:04.000
And while we're here, what do
these magical, mythical pins

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.760
look like on the file path?

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.620
It's not that special.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:13.000
So it's a hash of the
date, and the time,

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.590
and a particular hash
for the pin itself

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:17.610
and two things inside.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:22.680
Here's an RDS file with
my classic penguins data,

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.960
and here's a text file.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.462
It's just got metadata
included in it.

00:08:27.462 --> 00:08:33.030
So if I wanted to, I could
go and just go into my code,

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:37.289
and read that RDS file manually.

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:40.049
It's not once a
pin, always a pin.

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:41.049
It's just a thing.

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:42.990
It's just a
serializable thing in R.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.940
But pins is going
to make it easier

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.640
for me to find these
locations, work

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.500
with them in a
consistent manner,

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.320
pull them in, read them
in, and interpret and work

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.790
with this metadata overall.

00:08:58.932 --> 00:09:00.390
One of the first
things that you do

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:04.650
with pins is define your
board, and like I said,

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.080
your board can be on Connect.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.700
It can be on S3.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:13.780
It can be GitHub, or a folder
URL local, or whatever.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.690
So in this case,
just I like being

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.450
able to see what we're
doing while we're playing.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.280
So I'm creating this board.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.080
There's just a folder.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.650
This folder happens to
be my Dropbox pack here.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:29.340
But I can, to my
heart's content,

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:35.850
let's make another board
and make it versioned.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.030
We'll talk about
that in a second.

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:40.640
Board folder.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.570
Gosh, I have to type on this?

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:47.490
You know the autocomplete?

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:48.247
Dropbox.

00:09:48.247 --> 00:09:48.955
This is fabulous.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.040
So I've created two different
definitions for my board.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:07.010
This one's versioned.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.430
Sorry.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.070
This one is not versioned.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.250
This one is versioned.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.430
It's the same file path.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.950
I can have multiple boards.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.820
I'm just describing.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.790
With each definition
of board, I'm

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.650
just describing
where it is, how I'm

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.720
accessing it, and different
characteristics of it.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.280
So, for example, these two
different boards, regular board

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.630
and board versioned.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.200
I'm just saying, when I'm
using this file path, when

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:38.850
I'm using this board, either
do or don't automatically

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:41.310
keep multiple versions
of my pin for me.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:51.820
And so, there's different ways
for dealing with versions.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:55.270
You can define things at this
higher level on the board

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:55.900
level.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.330
It says, anything that
I pin to this board,

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:02.840
I want it automatically
to be versioned.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:08.230
And, for example, if you're
doing an RStudio Connect board,

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.650
by default, the RS
Connect board itself

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.150
is versioned [INAUDIBLE] true.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.490
Just out of the box default.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:19.330
But typically, the
versioning is false,

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:24.970
is turned off by default. And
if so, then you can also then do

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:28.500
versioning at the pin level.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:33.020
And so, you can see down
here as I've defined my pin--

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.270
We're getting
ahead of ourselves.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:36.200
Defined my pin.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.500
I can specify, even if
my board isn't versioned,

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.660
I can make my
specific pin versioned

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:46.385
and then have different
iterations of it.

00:11:46.385 --> 00:11:47.420
That's really cool.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:56.285
Caching is another nice
feature that, once you pull--

00:11:56.285 --> 00:12:00.470
once you get it set up,
you've pulled in your pin.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.910
There will be a cached
version of that.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:07.820
And so, it will only
pull a fresh version

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.130
if the data has
changed, and so, it

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.880
can be a time saver
utilizing that cache overall.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.490
Are there some limits?

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.490
Can I just pin everything?

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:22.270
No.

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:23.680
No.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.027
That would be irresponsible.

00:12:27.027 --> 00:12:29.360
You need to think a little
bit about what's appropriate,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.260
so there's two elements.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:36.910
What's appropriate in terms
of what are you trying to pin?

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:39.670
Is it something that maybe
belongs in a database?

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:50.620
Don't use pins for long-term
archival storage of data.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:54.370
Things like your
crown jewel data.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:55.990
Don't make that a pin.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.640
That needs to go in a database.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:01.450
There are best practices
around data, and databases,

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:05.160
and backing things up, and
access all of these things.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.630
Don't replace your
databases with pins.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:13.210
A good practice is to
make dependable objects

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:16.600
something that is reproducible.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.440
Don't count on your pin
being your source of truth

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:21.820
for all of eternity.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:24.070
Make sure it's something
that you can programmatically

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:26.950
reproduce.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.310
Pins are often really
good with ephemeral data.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:32.290
Stuff that's just
like coming in.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.060
They're in the
middle of my process.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.830
I need to stick this
somewhere and then be done--

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:38.530
keep working with it.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.020
Ephemeral data's really good,
really lightweight data.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.580
Lookup tables, stuff like that.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:44.770
Things that make
your life easier

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:47.410
is good to use for a pin.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:53.530
And then in terms of size, a
few hundred megabytes is great.

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:56.920
If it's too big for Excel,
it's too big to pin.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:00.280
Don't push it.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.670
So we talked about
boards already.

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:08.757
Boards, it's not magic.

00:14:08.757 --> 00:14:10.090
Think of it just as a file path.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.520
Where am I going?

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:17.710
And so, in this case, let's
just use this board here.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:21.700
That is my Dropbox location.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.855
Get rid of this one here.

00:14:23.855 --> 00:14:24.730
If I'm on the board--

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:28.030
So first of all, I can
just do a quick query.

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.170
What's out there?

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.740
So I'm going to
do board, and pins

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.660
is pipeable, which is nice.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:36.790
So you can work with it.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:40.480
I'll typically define my
board as my first step,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.890
and then pipe that into all
of my different functions.

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:44.710
So we'll pipe board
into pin list,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:48.280
and you can see here's all
of my pins that I have,

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.410
and this is mimicking.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:52.990
It's bringing in what you can
see here in my file browser.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:57.760
There's also if you're not
quite sure what's out there

00:14:57.760 --> 00:14:59.800
or if you want to
get more information,

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.060
pin_search is nice.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:06.760
pin_search will search the names
and the titles of your pens.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:10.150
So in this instance, I
don't have very many pins,

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:12.550
so I'm just going to
search for the blank query,

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:14.470
the double quotation marks.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.350
It does present in a nicer
format than just the list

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:18.762
that we got in the last one.

00:15:18.762 --> 00:15:20.470
You can see a little
bit more information

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:23.890
be created in the
file size and whatnot.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:27.050
And like I said, it will
search the name in the title.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:31.780
So if I'm looking for
penguins, I've got two pins.

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:35.380
I've got penguins, and
I have not penguins,

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:38.750
so searching those
different things.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.680
But this is good
for finding things.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.660
So pins has metadata
associated with them,

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.850
which can be pretty helpful.

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:48.970
So I've got this pin here.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:52.990
It's not super exciting,
but it's just a timestamp.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:55.990
But the metadata associated
with that, I can see what it is.

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:59.100
And in this case,
when I pinned it,

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:04.080
you can also include
your own custom metadata.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.870
So that can be very useful.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.670
As you're building
out a project or you

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.250
want to put specific
things into that metadata,

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:16.110
there is a place to put
that into the metadata.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:19.800
You can see how I put that in
up here as I wrote this pin,

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.500
and I just specified
the metadata as a list.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.180
I love that.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.880
And then different versions--
we've talked about versions.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.430
So I can see what
versions I have.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:31.740
So here are I pin
versions for timestamps.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:33.600
I've got five
different versions,

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:38.060
and let's just see for sanity.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.790
Over here in my
actual file browser,

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.970
here are my five
different versions.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:48.740
And you can see, it does
get to be duplicative.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:51.960
It's just copy, copy,
copy, one after another.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:57.830
So it's not the smartest
package in terms of file space.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:00.230
This is going back to this.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.210
Use pins if it makes
your life easier.

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:03.800
It's quick and
dirty, and it's there

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:07.280
to help you to get things done.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:09.994
Are there more elegant
ways of doing things?

00:17:09.994 --> 00:17:10.730
Sure.

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:14.210
Are there maybe better workflows
for long-term archival data

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:14.960
storage?

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:19.000
Absolutely, but
pins are definitely

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.365
useful in these use cases.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.339
So we've talked
about the pin_read

00:17:28.339 --> 00:17:30.860
and its complement,
the pin_write

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.560
and the pin_read functions.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:37.700
There is another set of
functions for pin_upload

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:39.860
and pin_download.

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:44.270
And so these take a little
bit of an abstraction layer

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:45.590
further out.

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:51.790
And really, this is for being
able to pin any kind of file,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:56.300
like an RData file, for example.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:57.880
So if you have an RData file--

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:02.840
again, here's my board,
my Dropbox board.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:05.430
I have this RData file.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:06.330
And what is it?

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:10.620
Right, it's just this collection
of my four different variables

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:13.570
and things that I
had for breakfast.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:19.760
So if I wanted to
put this on a board,

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.670
I'd use the pin_upload function.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:25.610
And pin_upload,
we can see what's

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.520
going to happen over here.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.880
I have this over here.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.380
And this is looking familiar.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.080
Here's my hash, but here's
my RData file itself.

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:37.460
No magic there.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:42.960
Here's my metadata, and it's
just tightly nested over here

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:44.930
in my file folder.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.380
And you'll note here,
it's replaced the version

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.330
that I had before as
opposed to appending

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:55.880
a version because I didn't tell
it I want a version for it.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:19:00.230
And so the complement to
pin_upload is pin_download.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:05.840
And what pin_download
does is it returns a path

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:10.130
to the cached file
that it's downloaded.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.330
So what does that look like?

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:14.530
Here's my output.

00:19:14.530 --> 00:19:16.240
Here's where it's living.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:21.210
Here's where it's downloaded
that file and cached it.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.430
And so I can now--

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:27.450
if I want to bring that
object in, pins board,

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:30.240
packed a cache pile,
I have my pack.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.320
And let's just load it.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:38.680
So you can use pin_upload
and pin_download to be

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:43.570
able to bring other objects,
files, things like that

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:46.060
into that structure
that pins provides

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:50.020
for being able to read
metadata, play with versions,

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.750
called things, have
caching, things like that.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:01.940
It's one of the legacy
functions in the API.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:07.670
And so what that means, so the
first iteration, I would say,

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:10.790
of the pins package had a
different overall format

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:13.520
and different API behind
it for doing the read

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:14.510
and write functions.

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:21.360
And so in-- gosh, maybe nine
months ago, maybe a year ago,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:22.206
I don't know.

00:20:22.206 --> 00:20:23.278
It depends on the time.

00:20:23.278 --> 00:20:24.320
It depends on the time.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.840
It doesn't make sense anymore.

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:33.870
A little while
ago, a new version,

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:37.460
a new API version
of pins came out,

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.480
and it started pins version 1.0.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.320
We're at 1.01 right
now, I believe.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:46.760
And that retooled
under the hood the API

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:50.840
that was calling, and
writing, and interacting

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.490
with pin boards.

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:57.510
And we also saw some of the
older functions drop off.

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.210
That being said
though, that doesn't

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:02.210
mean-- so Google Cloud doesn't
mean that that's not going

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:05.180
to be a board in the future.

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.160
There's currently
an issue right now

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:11.090
on the repo to bring
Google Cloud as one

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:15.200
of the new
implementations of boards,

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.270
and really, it's just
a matter of if that's

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:19.940
something that's
useful for folks,

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:24.650
we just need people to go in
and put a plus 1 on the issue

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:27.290
to help weigh the priority
for developing that.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:37.490
So we've talked about pinning
data, pinning data frames,

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:39.120
things like that.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.990
But pinning models is
a really nice workflow.

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:45.110
And all the work that
Julia's been doing around

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:50.730
vetiver I think showcases
that really nicely.

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:55.160
And so what you can do with
the package, with vetiver,

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:58.370
you can pin a vetiver model.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:01.760
And then with that, you'll
have all the information

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:05.690
about that model that you
need to deploy it, and call

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:06.990
it, and work with it.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.610
And so you can then
call that model

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:14.210
or different versions
of that model

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:17.960
into other assets downstream.

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:24.320
So a super simple example
of that, so earlier today--

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:26.240
let's see what I have.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:30.940
I borrowed some of
Julia's nice work here--

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.980
a model that she has
just demonstrated here

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:36.080
with home prices in Sacramento.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:41.840
She's using a random forest
model, and creating this model,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:45.960
saving it as a vetiver model.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.690
And if we do this
right-- let's actually

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:51.460
look at this, what
this looks like.

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:52.470
So what is v?

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:56.100
B is a ranger regression
modeling workflow

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.360
using core features.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:01.467
So with this, I'm
going to pin this.

00:23:01.467 --> 00:23:03.300
In this case again, I'm
going to just pin it

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:04.470
on my Dropbox folder.

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:08.940
So we'll pin this.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:14.917
And where's my notebook?

00:23:14.917 --> 00:23:16.500
So I have these
different models here.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:23.630
And now I'm going
to use this model.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:26.020
So first of all, one
of these workflows

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:31.120
that she's illustrated is to
wrap this model up into an API

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.390
so that you can pin
it and query it.

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:46.130
So I'll take this model, and
my background job here, there's

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:48.410
functions within
the vetiver package

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:52.170
for creating a vetiver API.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:57.180
In this case, I'm just
going to run this locally.

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.040
I could publish my API.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:01.000
I could publish it
somewhere hosted on Connect

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:02.542
or host it in some
other place that I

00:24:02.542 --> 00:24:03.680
have in my infrastructure.

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:06.000
Right now I'm just going
to run this locally

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.430
so I can think off of this.

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:13.970
And what this looks
like, so if you run this,

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:15.650
this is just running locally.

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:18.500
It has a nice swagger
interface so I can

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:21.230
test it out and try my model.

00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:24.170
So first, I'm going
to just do it.

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:27.480
This is just a ping,
and it's just responding

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.620
with the time of the day.

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:31.310
But if I want to
run this locally--

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:33.850
so I'm actually going to run
this as a background job,

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:35.830
because when this is running--

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:36.820
let me run this again.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.990
If this is running, it's
just consuming and occupying

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:44.000
my console, so I can't
interact with it.

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:48.290
A whole lot of good, that does.

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:51.660
I've got to run this
as a background job

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:55.020
so that I can interact and
still play with my console.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:25:00.760
So here's my background job,
and I'm going to run this.

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:02.420
And this is now running.

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:06.023
So I just go to a browser.

00:25:06.023 --> 00:25:07.315
Let's pull up a browser window.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.670
So here I'm at a
browser window, just

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.970
like we had at the preview, but
now I have a console window.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:24.500
And so I'm going to use my
model that's been pinned,

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:28.460
and I'm going to
define an endpoint.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:32.400
My endpoint is this
prediction endpoint here.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:35.677
And I'm going to
create some new data

00:25:35.677 --> 00:25:37.135
and then predict
off that endpoint.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:45.560
And so I just run predictions
off of that pin model

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:47.210
that I have hosted--

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:50.320
that I have running
[INAUDIBLE] in the background.

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:57.160
But what's nice is that in
this model that I'm running,

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:00.850
I remember I pulled it from
a pin I've got that in a pin.

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:02.410
I specified the version.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.690
So say I had a different
version of this model

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:07.420
that I wanted to
run, well, let's see.

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:08.600
Which versions do I have?

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:11.320
I've got three different
versions right here.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:17.860
I want to instead
grab this version.

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:20.110
All right, if I wanted
to use this version,

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.410
then I would just change
here which particular version

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:25.660
that I'd be reading
in from my pen

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:29.330
and serving up VMI API overall.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:32.950
And so this is a nice way that
you can toggle back and forth

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:36.690
and try different versions
of your pinned model.

